    "evidence_text": "OBJECTIVE To determine whether the use of statins is associated with reduced hip fracture risk.   \n\n\nMAIN OUTCOME MEASURE Adjusted odds ratio (OR) of hip fracture by statin use in the 180 days and 3 years prior to the index date (the earliest date of admission for surgery), adjusted for demographic and clinical characteristics and health care utilization.   \n\n\nNo significant relationship was observed between use of nonstatin lipid-lowering agents and hip fracture risk.\n\nClear relationships were observed between the degree of reduction in hip fracture risk and the extent of statin use; there was no evidence of such relationships with nonstatin lipid-lowering agents.\n\nThe relationship between statin use and hip fracture risk persisted after controlling for variables such as the number of medications, the Charlson comorbidity index score, and hospitalization or nursing home stay in the last 180 days, as well as after excluding patients who were in a nursing home prior to their index date or who died in the year after their index date.\n\nCONCLUSIONS These findings support an association between statin use by elderly patients and reduction in the risk of hip fracture.\n\nJAMA.\n\nSecondly, long-term statin use might cause adverse effects, such as incident diabetes mellitus.",
